$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{3-\sqrt{5x-1}}{x-2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{3-\sqrt{5x-1}}{x-2}\cdot\dfrac{3+\sqrt{5x-1}}{3+\sqrt{5x-1}}\qquad\gray{\text{Rationalize the numerator}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{3^2-(5x-1)}{(x-2)(3+\sqrt{5x-1})}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-5\cancel{(x-2)}}{\cancel{(x-2)}(3+\sqrt{5x-1})}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel out common factors}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-5}{3+\sqrt{5x-1}} \text{, for }x\neq 2
\end{align}$

This means that the two expressions have the same value for all $x$-values (in their domains) except for $2$.

We can now use the following theorem:

If $f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$-values in a given interval except for $x=c$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=\lim_{x\to c}g(x)$.
